The answer depends entirely on what you mean by "AI."

There are really four different limits in Unity:

System	Approximate Modern Limit

Traditional MonoBehaviour AI	100–500 active NPCs
Optimized GameObject AI	500–2,000 NPCs
DOTS/ECS AI	10,000–100,000+ simple agents
GPU/Simulation AI	100,000–1,000,000+ agents


Unity's current DOTS/ECS stack is specifically designed for massive simulation and can scale to extremely large entity counts when using ECS, Burst, and Jobs correctly. Unity itself advertises ECS as enabling "unprecedented simulation scale" and large-scale deterministic simulations. 

The Real Bottleneck Isn't AI

Most developers assume AI is expensive.

Usually it isn't.

The expensive parts are:

Animation

Pathfinding

Physics

Rendering

Networking

Memory bandwidth


A simple AI state machine:

Idle
Move
Attack
Retreat

can run on tens of thousands of entities.

The moment you add:

NavMesh

Full skeleton animation

Ragdolls

Vision cones

Behavior trees

Real physics


the number drops dramatically.


